==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*newun}}, from {{inh|ang|ine-pro|*h₁néwn̥||nine}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|nigun}} ({{cog|ny|njoggen}}), {{cog|osx|nigun}} ({{cog|nl|negen}}), together representing a variant of the original Germanic (with /ɡ/ for /w/), seen in {{cog|goh|niun}} ({{cog|de|neun}}),{{cog|non|níu}} ({{cog|sv|nio}}), {{cog|got|𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌽}}. The PIE source also led to {{cog|la|novem}}, {{cog|sq|nëntë}}, {{cog|sga|noí}} ({{cog|ga|naoi}}), {{cog|sla-pro|*devętь}} ({{cog|ru|девять}}).

